============
Installing GMP or compiling it from source
============

# This has only been tested on Ubuntu 26.04
# This is valid as of 2026, libraries may change

Easy way:
sudo apt install libgmp-dev

# Install the compiler, etc.
sudo apt install build-essential
sudo apt install m4

# (First check https://gmplib.org to see if there's a more recent version)
wget https://gmplib.org/download/gmp/gmp-6.3.0.tar.xz
tar xJf gmp-6.3.0.tar.xz
cd gmp-6.3.0

####################
NOTE on Ubuntu 26.04, you must change lines lines 6571 and 8190 of the configure script from:
void g(){}
to
void g(int, const t1 *, int, t1 *, const t1 *, int){}

due to C23 standard's strictness regarding empty parameter lists
####################

Then do:
./configure

# Make sure it doesn't default to nehalem-pc-linux-gnu because it doesn't know anything more recent
# Can do, for example: ./configure --build=alderlake-pc-linux-gnu

# Can also do:
./configure --build=x86_64-pc-linux-gnu CFLAGS="-O3 -march=native" CXXFLAGS="-O3 -march=native"

# Can also do:
./configure --enable-fat


make
make check
sudo make install



Check for /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libgmp.so to see if libgmp-dev is installed
Might also be /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libgmp.so
    Maybe do:
        sudo apt remove libgmp-dev
    But instead might first do:
        /sbin/ldconfig -v 2>/dev/null | grep -E '^[^[:space:]]'
        or
        /sbin/ldconfig -v 2>/dev/null | grep -v ^$'\t'
    to see what the library path is, and see whether /usr/local/lib or /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu has priority

    And do:
        ldd <executable>
    to see which shared libraries it will actually use

Or set LD_LIBRARY_PATH environment variable at runtime to override system defaults
